 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$, where $N_{iters}$ is iterations, $N_{rand}$ is batch size, $N_{samples}$ is samples per ray, and $D$ is MLP dimensionality [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$ for the forward pass, plus optimizer state $O(|\Theta|)$ [S5, unofficial]. VRAM is managed by chunking rays [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Trained for 100,000–1,000,000 iterations [S11, S14, unofficial]. Typical batch size 1024–2048 [S5, S11, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; effective for dense-view, static scenes; differentiable end-to-end.
-*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence for high-frequency details; prone to "floaters" in empty space; requires significant VRAM for large scenes without acceleration [S1][S17, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Baseline for novel view synthesis; when computational resources allow for long training times; when ground-truth multi-view images are available.
-*   **When Not to Use:** Sparse-view settings (use [Sparse View and Initialization Methods](9394-sparse-view-and-initialization-methods.md)); real-time applications (use [Acceleration and Sparse Structures](11405-acceleration-and-sparse-structures.md)); dynamic scenes (use [Progressive and Dynamic Scene Growth](8869-progressive-and-dynamic-scene-growth.md)).
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$, where $D$ is the MLP dimensionality. The dominant cost is the neural network query per sample point [S5, S14, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$ for the forward pass activations, plus $O(N_{iters})$ for optimizer states (Adam stores two moments per parameter) [S5, 44-radiance-field-optimization.md, unofficial]. Chunking reduces peak memory usage during the forward pass [S5, S7, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Training typically requires 100,000 to 1,000,000 iterations [S11, S14, unofficial]. On standard datasets (Blender, LLFF), convergence is observed within this range [S11, S14, unofficial]. Batch sizes of 1024-2048 rays are common [S5, S11, S14, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with the number of training views and image resolution. Larger batch sizes improve throughput but may hinder data loading performance and GPU occupancy if too large; smaller batch sizes cap iterations per second [S15, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; leverages standard deep learning frameworks; produces high-quality novel views for interpolation [S5, S14, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Slow training due to repeated MLP queries; prone to overfitting on sparse views without regularization; struggles with view extrapolation [S1, S24].
+*   **When to Use:** Standard novel view synthesis from dense multi-view images.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time training is required, or when dealing with extreme sparsity without additional priors/regularization.
 
 ## Background
-The NeRF Training Loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S5, unofficial]. It established the standard for differentiable volume rendering optimization, using an MLP to map 3D coordinates and viewing directions to density and color, optimized via MSE loss and Adam [S5, S11, unofficial]. It has since become the foundational method for numerous extensions, including mip-NeRF [S21], Instant Neural Graphics Primitives (NGP) [S15, unofficial], and various sparse-view and dynamic scene variants [S1][S2, unofficial].
+The NeRF Training Loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S5, unofficial][S20]. It builds on the concept of implicit neural representations for 3D scenes [S3, unofficial][S20]. The method uses a coordinate-based MLP to map 3D positions and viewing directions to volume density and color, optimized via stochastic gradient descent on pixel-wise MSE loss [S5, S14, unofficial]. It has become a foundational technique in 3D computer vision, inspiring numerous variants for acceleration, sparse views, and dynamic scenes [S1, S24].
 
